\section{Method}\label{sec:method}

\subsection{Preliminaries}\label{sec:background}

\paragraph{Goal Accessibility Ratio.}
We denote the task specification tokens in the system prompt as \emph{goal tokens} $\mathcal{G}$. \citet{dongre2026attention} defined the Goal Accessibility Ratio (GAR) for a model with $L$ layers and $H$ heads at turn $\tau$ as
\begin{equation}\label{eq:gar}
  \text{GAR}(\tau) = \frac{1}{L \cdot H \cdot |\mathcal{R}_\tau|} \sum_{\ell=1}^{L} \sum_{h=1}^{H} \sum_{i \in \mathcal{R}_\tau} \sum_{j \in \mathcal{G}} A_{i,j}^{(\ell,h)},
\end{equation}
where $\mathcal{R}_\tau$ is the set of response tokens at turn $\tau$ and $A_{i,j}^{(\ell,h)}$ is the attention weight from token $i$ to token $j$ at layer $\ell$, head $h$.

\paragraph{Two GAR variants.}
\textbf{GAR-Dongre} is the original definition (Eq.~\ref{eq:gar}), averaging over all response tokens. \textbf{GAR-ours} restricts the measurement to the \emph{last token} of each assistant turn:
\begin{equation}\label{eq:gar-ours}
  \text{GAR\text{-}ours}(\tau) = \frac{1}{L \cdot H} \sum_{\ell=1}^{L} \sum_{h=1}^{H} \sum_{j \in \mathcal{G}} A_{t_\tau,\, j}^{(\ell,h)},
\end{equation}
where $t_\tau$ is the last token index at turn $\tau$. Attention at this position reflects the information drawn upon for continuation; we use GAR-ours as the optimization target for $\mathcal{L}_\text{GAR}$ (\S\ref{sec:loss}).

\subsection{ThreadGate Architecture}

ThreadGate adds a learned gating mechanism to the transformer's residual stream. At each layer $l$, the residual update is modulated by a scalar gate:
\begin{equation}
  x_{l+1} = x_l + g_l \cdot f_l(x_l),
\end{equation}
where $f_l$ denotes the sublayer computation (self-attention or FFN). This resembles ReZero \citep{bachlechner2021rezero}, which initializes a single trainable scalar at zero to stabilize deep network training. ThreadGate differs in three ways: (i)~gates are \emph{content-conditioned}---a function of the hidden state, not a fixed scalar; (ii)~gates are \emph{turn-conditioned}---incorporating conversation-position features; (iii)~gates are initialized near identity ($\sigma(1.5) \approx 0.82$) rather than zero, preserving the pretrained model's computation.

\paragraph{Gate network.} Each gate $g_l$ is produced by a two-layer MLP:
\begin{align}\label{eq:gate}
  g_l = \sigma\bigl(W_2\,\text{ReLU}(&W_1\,[\text{proj}(x_l);\;\text{emb}(t_d, t_i)] \notag\\
  &+ b_1) + b_2\bigr),
\end{align}
where $\text{proj}(\cdot)$ linearly projects from the model's hidden dimension ($d\!=\!3584$ for Qwen-2.5-7B) to 16 dimensions, and $\text{emb}(t_d, t_i)$ produces a 16-dimensional turn embedding. The concatenated 32-dimensional input passes through a 64-unit hidden layer to produce a scalar gate value in $(0, 1)$ via the sigmoid $\sigma$. Each of the $L$ transformer layers receives its own gate network; for Qwen-2.5-7B ($L\!=\!28$), this adds ${\sim}$57K parameters ($<$0.001\% of 7B), making the overhead negligible.

\paragraph{Turn embedding.} The embedding $\text{emb}(t_d, t_i)$ encodes two features via learned lookup tables: \emph{turn distance} $t_d$ (number of turns since the system prompt, capturing relative position in the conversation) and \emph{turn index} $t_i$ (absolute turn number). This dual encoding allows gates to distinguish between early and late conversation turns even when hidden states are similar. The \textbf{Uniform Gate} ablation (\S\ref{sec:experiments}) replaces $\text{emb}(\cdot)$ with a learnable constant vector, preserving gate capacity while removing turn awareness.

\paragraph{Initialization.} Gate biases are set to 1.5, so the model begins by passing ${\sim}$82\% of each sublayer's output through the residual stream. This contrasts with ReZero's zero initialization, which would effectively suppress all sublayer contributions and destroy the pretrained model's capabilities. Gate parameters receive a $10\times$ learning rate multiplier relative to LoRA adapters.

\subsection{Training Objective}\label{sec:loss}

The training objective combines three terms:
\begin{equation}
  \mathcal{L} = \mathcal{L}_\text{NTP} + \lambda_\text{GAR}\,\mathcal{L}_\text{GAR} + \lambda_\text{div}\,\mathcal{L}_\text{div}.
\end{equation}
$\mathcal{L}_\text{NTP}$ is the standard next-token prediction loss. The GAR loss targets a fixed attention level to goal tokens:
\begin{equation}
  \mathcal{L}_\text{GAR} = \text{MSE}(\text{GAR\text{-}ours}, \tau),
\end{equation}
where $\tau = 0.5$. GAR is measured at the last token of each assistant turn (GAR-ours, \S\ref{sec:background}). The gate diversity loss prevents collapse to a uniform value:
\begin{equation}
  \mathcal{L}_\text{div} = \text{ReLU}(\sigma_\text{min} - \text{std}(\mathbf{g})),
\end{equation}
where $\mathbf{g} = [g_1, \ldots, g_L]$ and $\sigma_\text{min}$ is a minimum standard deviation threshold. Both $\lambda_\text{GAR} = \lambda_\text{div} = 1.0$.

\paragraph{Loss design evolution.} The MSE formulation emerged from seven iterations spanning four loss families (Table~\ref{tab:loss-iterations}, Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix-loss}). Cumulative losses (v1--v2) collapsed to zero once the GAR threshold was reached, starving gate gradients at equilibrium. Maximize-family losses (v3--v5) drove gates toward identity ($g \to 1.0$) because the unbounded objective rewards full pass-through, eliminating layer selectivity. Smooth saturation (v6) bounded the reward but followed the same terminal trajectory. Only the MSE formulation provides bidirectional pressure---penalizing both over- and under-attention---which, combined with the diversity regularizer, prevents identity collapse and enforces layer-selective gating.

\subsection{Controlled Comparison Design}\label{sec:comparison-design}

To identify why gating fails to transfer from training to inference, we design a five-way comparison where each adjacent pair isolates exactly one factor:

\begin{itemize}\setlength{\itemsep}{2pt}
  \item \textbf{ThreadGate vs.\ Uniform Gate}: Uniform Gate replaces the turn embedding with a learnable constant, removing turn awareness while preserving content conditioning and gate capacity. Difference isolates \emph{turn conditioning}.
  \item \textbf{ThreadGate vs.\ LoRA-only}: LoRA-only fixes all gates at 1.0 and trains with $\mathcal{L}_\text{NTP} + \mathcal{L}_\text{GAR}$ only. Difference isolates the \emph{gating mechanism}.
  \item \textbf{ThreadGate vs.\ +PCGrad}: +PCGrad applies gradient surgery \citep{yu2020pcgrad} to gate parameters, projecting conflicting NTP and GAR gradients onto compatible directions. Difference isolates \emph{gradient direction conflict}.
  \item \textbf{Fine-tuned vs.\ Base}: Base is the pretrained model without fine-tuning. Difference isolates \emph{LoRA adaptation}.
\end{itemize}

All fine-tuned variants share training data (8K UltraChat conversations), LoRA configuration (rank 16, $\alpha$=32 on \texttt{q\_proj}/\texttt{v\_proj}), optimizer (AdamW, lr $= 2 \times 10^{-4}$), and seed (42), differing only in the factor under test (Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix-hyper}).

\section{Introduction}\label{sec:introduction}

Multi-turn dialogue is the dominant mode of interaction with large language models (LLMs), and system prompts that specify task goals form the foundation of instruction following. As conversations grow longer, however, models progressively lose track of these goal specifications. \citet{dongre2026attention} quantified this degradation across 12 models: the Goal Accessibility Ratio (GAR), which measures how much attention the model allocates to goal tokens in the system prompt, declines by 27--48\% between early and mid-conversation turns. We replicate and extend this finding on Qwen-2.5-7B-Instruct, observing a 70.8\% relative decline in GAR from 0.0418 at turn~1 to 0.0122 at mid-conversation. We attribute the steeper decline partly to our use of last-token GAR, which captures summary-position attention more sensitively than the all-token average of \citet{dongre2026attention}, and partly to Qwen-2.5-7B's stronger instruction-following fidelity in early turns. This attention decay represents a systematic failure of multi-turn models to maintain task coherence, and it grows worse with each additional turn.

A striking aspect of this degradation is what it does \emph{not} affect. \citet{dongre2026attention} showed that goal-relevant information persists in the residual stream throughout the conversation: linear discriminant analysis probes recover goal content with AUC\,=\,0.98 even at late turns where attention to goal tokens has collapsed. The information is present in the hidden states, but the attention mechanism fails to route it toward the output computation. This dissociation between information availability and information utilization suggests a natural intervention point.

If the residual stream already carries goal information, a learnable gate on the residual pathway could amplify or attenuate layer contributions to recover goal attention. This reasoning motivates ThreadGate, which modulates each residual update with a content-and-turn-conditioned MLP gate: $x_{l+1} = x_l + g_l \cdot f_l(x_l)$, where $g_l$ is produced by a two-layer network conditioned on the hidden state and turn features, inspired by the residual scaling idea of ReZero \citep{bachlechner2021rezero}.

\begin{figure*}[t]
  \centering
  \includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{fig1_overview.pdf}
  \caption{Overview of ThreadGate and its failure mode. (Left) ThreadGate adds per-layer content-and-turn-conditioned MLP gates to the residual stream, trained jointly with an auxiliary GAR loss that targets goal attention. (Right) Under teacher-forced evaluation, GAR-ours reaches 0.50 across all fine-tuned variants; under autoregressive generation, it drops to 0.019--0.064, an 8--17$\times$ gap. Turn-conditioned gating (ThreadGate) significantly outperforms LoRA-only ($p$\,=\,0.004) but the residual gap remains large (7.8$\times$).}
  \label{fig:overview}
\end{figure*}

ThreadGate combines per-layer MLP gate networks with a joint training objective: next-token prediction (NTP), an auxiliary GAR loss ($\tau = 0.5$), and a gate diversity loss (\S\ref{sec:method}). The final formulation emerged from seven iterations spanning four loss families; only MSE target with gate diversity converged stably. To diagnose the failure, we construct a five-way controlled comparison (ThreadGate, +PCGrad, Uniform Gate, LoRA-only, Base) where each pair isolates one factor (\S\ref{sec:experiments}). PCGrad \citep{yu2020pcgrad} serves a dual role: training strategy and exclusion tool that rules out gradient direction conflict.

Training succeeds: teacher-forced GAR-ours reaches 0.50 across all fine-tuned variants. The five-way comparison reveals that the failure is specific to autoregressive generation, where GAR-ours drops to 0.019--0.064, an 8--17$\times$ gap. Turn-conditioned gating (ThreadGate) significantly outperforms LoRA-only ($p$\,=\,0.004, GAR-ours 0.064 vs 0.037), yet the residual gap remains large (7.8$\times$). The PCGrad exclusion experiment eliminates gradient direction conflict without altering any outcome, establishing that conflict is neither necessary nor sufficient for the failure. These contrasts converge on exposure bias \citep{bengio2015scheduled}, where attention patterns optimized under teacher-forced prefixes do not transfer to model-generated text (Figure~\ref{fig:overview}). An inference-time steering experiment provides constructive evidence that attention misallocation is a proximal cause: additive attention bias recovers autoregressive GAR by $>$11$\times$, bypassing the exposure bias barrier (\S\ref{subsec:steering}). A preliminary scheduled sampling experiment confirms that the gap is also mitigable at training time: GAR-ours increases from 0.064 to 0.347 (5.4$\times$), compressing the 7.8$\times$ gap to 1.44$\times$ with no PPL cost (single seed; \S\ref{sec:discussion}).

Our contributions: (1)~a systematic diagnostic framework for analyzing auxiliary objective failures in multi-turn dialogue, comprising a five-way controlled comparison that isolates four factors (turn conditioning, gating mechanism, gradient direction conflict, and fine-tuning), PCGrad-based exclusion via gradient surgery \citep{yu2020pcgrad}, and per-layer gradient conflict quantification (\S\ref{sec:method}, \S\ref{sec:experiments}, \S\ref{sec:analysis}); (2)~an empirical characterization of how exposure bias selectively destroys auxiliary attention loss generalization (8--17$\times$ gap) while leaving NTP robust, with evidence that token-level self-recovery \citep{he2019exposure} does not extend to auxiliary objectives, a preliminary demonstration that scheduled sampling compresses this gap from 7.8$\times$ to 1.44$\times$ (single seed), and cross-model confirmation on Llama-3.1-8B (27$\times$ gap); (3)~an inference-time steering experiment: additive attention bias at $\beta$\,=\,5 recovers autoregressive GAR from 0.064 to 0.727, providing constructive evidence that attention misallocation---not representation degradation---is a proximal cause under the tested conditions (Qwen-2.5-7B and Llama-3.1-8B, UltraChat, GAR-family losses; \S\ref{subsec:steering}); (4)~a principled negative baseline: seven loss configurations across four families (Table~\ref{tab:loss-iterations}; Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix-loss}), of which only MSE converges, and a five-way comparison ruling out four alternative training strategies (\S\ref{sec:method}, \S\ref{sec:experiments}).

